bangladesh is one of the world's largest  tea producers the industry employs more  than 150 000 people at more than 200  plantations  most of the workers are female they work  long hours and earn some of the lowest  wages in the country  luchi kandu and her husband both work on  a plantation they say little has changed  for tea workers over the generations  we hardly get any type of facilities  don't have enough money for our  children's education we barely get three  kilograms of flour as rations once a  week some days we don't even get to eat  which is why we are protesting  bangladesh export tea to more than 20  countries including the us uk and  tea france want their minimum wage to be  raised they earn slightly more than a  dollar a day now  we have been protesting for 12 days now  the owners don't understand our play we  have been demanding our daily wage be  raised to 3 a day then we will go back  to work  plantation owners say they are going  through difficult times with profits  declining in recent years  in reality what they are saying is not  right we provide a medical fund  retirement benefit along with weekly  rations and access to primary education  for the children it all adds up to  around four dollars a day  late on saturday the government proposed  a wage hike of 25 cents a day but it was  rejected by the workers  [Music]  the administration has used intimidation  and threats to pressure the workers  union to accept the government's latest  proposal of a pay hike but since the tea  garden students organization and workers  have rejected it and protested now the  union has also rejected it  the stories of these workers may sound  like myths to many but it's their daily  reality the tea workers and their unions  have pledged to continue their strike  until their demand for pay hike is met  the un says they are one of the most  marginalized groups in the country with  limited access to basic facilities and  education  recent strikes by the tea workers of  bangladesh have become a railing point  for many here as rising inflation and  high food prices add to the wider  frustration about low wages chanwi  choudhary al-jazeera sri mango
 

